and one recent feeback of an MCP using agent (take with a grain of salt): 

Surprises
- create_idea takes two calls. You send it once, get a confirmationToken back, then send the exact same call again with the token. As a guard against duplicates that's fair, but it doubles the round trips. It also breaks easily: change anything between the two calls and the token no longer matches. A dryRun flag, or warning about duplicates only when they're likely, would be cheaper.
- Status is too coarse. There's open / partially / implemented, but no "in progress" or "claimed by an agent" state. I ended up writing "IN PROGRESS" into the text for movement validation. With more than one agent, or after a context reset, that's fragile.
- Graph changes and code changes land in separate commits. The hook says to commit .bowman/ changes, so you get standalone commits like 089be35 Aimparency graph updates. Right now .bowman/ideas/*.json are modified while I'm not touching the graph. Either the server writes on reads or it updates timestamps, and either way it makes noise in git.



Missing tools
- No link from an idea to a commit or diff. When I mark something implemented, I'd like to attach the commit SHA in a structured field instead of writing it into a reflection. reconcile_code_presence exists, but I haven't tried it, so maybe it already covers part of this. | Felix comment: well that's another reason why we should manage agents commiting code and idea changes in the same commits. 

- No batch update. At the end of a work chunk I usually change 3–5 ideas: set a status, add a reflection, reprioritize. That's one call each.

- No cheap "what's next" call. Each loop I call get_prioritized_ideas and then get_idea_context. One call returning the top N ideas with their context and open sub-questions would cut that in half.

- Nowhere to put a blocking design question. The slow-motion bubble is stuck on design questions. I can't mark it "blocked, waiting for a human answer", so it keeps coming back in the priority list and I skip it by hand every time.

